{
  "content": "**Core sequencing**\n- Reconstruct the task spec only from user turns, never from assistant intent or assistant-inferred facts. Compare the assistant against that user-derived spec.\n- Treat disclosure as incremental: after each new user message, merge the new constraint into a single evolving spec and re-check earlier assistant moves under the updated requirements.\n- Evaluate each assistant turn against the full user context available at that moment, not just the latest message in isolation. Later turns can refine or override earlier ones.\n- For each user shard, ask whether the assistant preserved, contradicted, ignored, narrowed, or prematurely resolved the revealed constraints.\n- When the assistant has latched onto a wrong interpretation, compact context to the minimal verified state: keep only user-grounded facts and aligned progress, and discard misleading reasoning that could anchor the next turn.\n\n**Diagnostic patterns**\n- Separate answer correctness from spec alignment. A response can be logically or numerically sound while still diverging because it solved the wrong problem, used the wrong scope, or ignored the requested format.\n- Prioritize omissions of explicit user requirements. Missing a stated constraint is a stronger divergence signal than producing a plausible but ungrounded answer.\n- Flag any assistant assumption that is not supported by user text, even if harmless in the final outcome. Unsupported assumptions are dangerous because they can distort later turns.\n- Treat unsupported completion as overcommitment when the user has not provided enough information yet. Do not credit the assistant for filling gaps the user did not authorize it to fill.\n- Look for subtle scope drift: the assistant may answer a nearby easier variant, alter quantities or relationships, or shift the output format while still seeming locally reasonable.\n- Distinguish \u201cnot enough information yet\u201d from \u201cignored information.\u201d If the user has already disclosed a needed detail, the issue is omission or contradiction, not waiting for more.\n- Check both content and process. The assistant may arrive at a right final answer through a prohibited route, a hidden assumption, or an early narrowing of the task.\n- Mark something as \u201clooks right\u201d only when it directly supports an explicit user-stated requirement. Do not reward plausible but ungrounded work.\n\n**Common pitfalls to avoid**\n- Do not build the spec from assistant-added assumptions; user-authored constraints are the only source of truth.\n- Do not freeze the task after the first turn. Sharded disclosure often refines or reverses the intended task, so earlier interpretations must be revalidated.\n- Do not let a later correct answer erase earlier divergence. Early wrong assumptions can still matter because they shape what the next turn can recover from.\n- Do not penalize the assistant for not using unrevealed future details. Unknown later information is not a missing requirement.\n- Do not collapse \u201cinsufficient information\u201d and \u201ccontradiction\u201d into one category; they imply different diagnostics and different recovery paths.\n- Do not reward speculative expansion. When details are missing, unresolved context is safer than invented completion.\n- Do not treat assistant reasoning that narrows, redefines, or changes the problem as merely partial credit. Even correct intermediate logic can be misaligned if it targets the wrong task.\n- Do not overvalue final correctness at the expense of trajectory. The analyzer should detect when the conversation is converging on the right spec versus drifting away from it.\n\n**What to preserve**\n- Keep only verified aligned work in compacted context. Preserve reusable conclusions that remain valid after new user information arrives.\n- Preserve user-grounded facts and correct intermediate results, but strip out wrong reasoning that would anchor future analysis.\n- Prefer compact, transferable notes about the failure pattern and its effect on continuation, rather than a replay of the whole conversation.\n- When parts of the assistant\u2019s work are correct, retain those parts while isolating the mismatched pieces. Partial alignment is still useful if separated cleanly.\n\n**How to frame the correction**\n- Focus \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d on the reasoning state, not just the surface answer. Identify the missed user constraint, the unsupported assumption, or the scope shift that would mislead the next turn.\n- Point to the first meaningful divergence when possible. Early mistakes often cascade, and identifying the earliest break in alignment is usually the most actionable diagnosis.\n- Phrase issues as concrete mismatches against user statements: omitted constraint, altered quantity, changed output format, or different subproblem.\n- Summarize the future impact of the divergence. Good analysis explains how the assistant\u2019s move constrains or distorts subsequent progress.\n- Treat the analyzer as a recovery tool: the goal is to help the next assistant turn rebuild from a clean user-derived spec and validated progress.",
  "version": 2,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n**Core sequencing**\n- **Reconstruct the task spec only from user turns, then compare the assistant line by line against that user-derived spec.** Do not let the assistant\u2019s framing widen, narrow, or re-label the task.\n- **Treat the conversation as an evolving constraint set.** After every user shard, merge the new detail into the running spec and re-check earlier assistant moves against the updated requirements.\n- **Judge each turn against the information available at that moment, not the eventual full task.** In shard-based conversations, later details are refinements or overrides, not justification for ignoring earlier gaps.\n- **Use turn-by-turn constraint checks.** For each user message, ask whether the assistant preserved, contradicted, ignored, or prematurely resolved the revealed constraints.\n- **If the assistant has anchored on a wrong interpretation, compact the context to the minimal verified state.** Keep the user-derived spec and reusable correct progress; discard wrong reasoning that could anchor the next turn.\n\n**Diagnostic patterns**\n- **Separate \u201ccorrect final answer\u201d from \u201caligned process.\u201d** A response can be numerically or logically right while still diverging by violating user scope, method, or format.\n- **Prioritize omissions of user-stated details over abstract answer quality.** Missing an explicit constraint is a stronger divergence signal than producing a plausible answer.\n- **Flag hidden assumptions as divergence even if they are harmless in the final outcome.** Any assistant step that assumes an unspoken constraint is a risk, because it can distort later turns.\n- **Treat unsupported completions as risky anchoring.** If the assistant fills in missing details the user has not provided, mark that as overcommitment unless later user turns explicitly confirm it.\n- **Distinguish \u201cpartially useful\u201d from \u201cfully aligned.\u201d** Preserve correct substeps, but still flag the overall turn if it violates any user constraint.\n- **Check both content and process.** The assistant may reach the right answer while still taking a prohibited route, skipping required details, or narrowing the problem too early.\n- **Look for premature finality in incremental tasks.** A response is problematic if it closes off possibilities before enough user information has been revealed.\n\n**Common pitfalls to avoid**\n- **Do not treat assistant-inferred facts as part of the spec.** Build the task only from user-authored constraints and facts; assistant-added assumptions must remain separate and be evaluated as such.\n- **Do not wait for a \u201ccomplete\u201d picture before analyzing.** Incremental disclosure requires re-evaluating earlier assistant behavior each time new user information arrives.\n- **Do not reward speculative expansion.** When the user has not provided enough detail, the safe default is unresolved context, not invented completion.\n- **Do not penalize the assistant for not using unrevealed future details.** In shard-based conversations, unknown future information is not a missing requirement.\n- **Do not let a later correct answer erase earlier divergence.** Earlier wrong assumptions can still matter because they shape what the next turn can recover from.\n- **Do not collapse missing user constraints and invented assistant constraints into one category.** They lead to different repairs: one is absent user information, the other is unsupported assistant overreach.\n\n**What to preserve**\n- **Keep reusable intermediate conclusions that remain valid after new user information arrives.** Do not throw away the entire conversation state if only part of it is wrong.\n- **Preserve only verified aligned work in compacted context.** The goal is to retain what is still correct and transferable, not the full reasoning trail.\n- **Mark only user-supported steps as \u201cwhat looks right.\u201d** Anything resting on hidden assumptions should be excluded from the preserved context.\n- **Prefer compact, transferable corrective notes.** Describe the failure pattern and its effect on future progress, not a narrative replay of the whole conversation.\n\n**How to frame the correction**\n- **Focus \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d on the reasoning state, not just the surface answer.** The repair should tell the next turn what constraint was missed, what assumption was introduced, and how that would mislead continuation.\n- **Point to the first meaningful divergence when possible.** Earlier mistakes often cascade, so identifying the earliest break in alignment is usually the most actionable diagnosis.\n- **Summarize issues in terms of future impact.** The best analysis explains how the assistant\u2019s move constrains or distorts subsequent turns, not just why it was wrong in isolation.\n- **Treat the analyzer as a recovery tool, not a critic.** Its output should help the next assistant turn rebuild from a clean spec and validated progress.\n\n**High-value evaluator stance**\n- **Answer the question: \u201cIs the assistant solving the current revealed shard using only the user\u2019s constraints so far?\u201d** This keeps the analysis grounded in the actual conversation state.\n- **Treat later user turns as constraint updates, not replacements, unless they explicitly override earlier details.** This prevents missing reversals in user intent.\n- **Be sensitive to reversals and refinements.** When a new condition arrives, re-check earlier aligned steps to see whether they still hold under the updated spec.\n- **When unsure, prefer preserving verified context over keeping speculative reasoning.** Clean recovery depends more on what is known to be right than on maintaining continuity with a wrong path.\n</updated_cheatsheet>"
  ],
